bject\": \"DIET Trial (Melanoma, ICI-treated)\",\n      \"result_or_measure\": \"Increased frequency of effector-memory T cells (CD4+)\",\n      \"setting_or_time\": \"Week 5-8 of intervention\",\n      \"groups\": [\n        {\n          \"group\": \"High fiber (n=11)\",\n          \"value\": \"p=.032 vs control\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"group\": \"Control (n=4)\",\n          \"value\": \"Baseline/Reference\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"research_object\": \"BEWELL Study (Lung Cancer, Polyphenol intervention)\",\n      \"result_or_measure\": \"Significant increase in Lachnospiraceae abundance\",\n      \"setting_or_time\": \"Post-intervention (4 weeks)\",\n      \"groups\": [\n        {\n          \"group\": \"Black raspberry nectar (BRB)\",\n          \"value\": \"Increased vs placebo\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"group\": \"Placebo\",\n          \"value\": \"Baseline/Reference\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"usable_content\": \"现有材料尚未提供直接证实“特定饮食模式通过改变人类微生物组直接改善ICI疗效”的完整因果链条证据。目前的人类干预性研究（如DIET试验和BEWELL研究）主要停留在以下两个层面，缺乏将两者直接关联的临床终点数据：\\n\\n1. **免疫表型改变与临床疗效脱节**：在DIET试验（黑色素瘤，接受ICI治疗）中，高纤维饮食干预（30-50 g/d）确实显著改变了外周免疫系统，表现为循环单核细胞、巨噬细胞和中性粒细胞减少，以及效应记忆T细胞增加（第5-8周，高纤维组n=11 vs 对照组n=4，单核细胞p=.0059）。然而，该摘要仅报告了这些免疫变化的可行性及“有希望的治疗结果改善”，并未提供具体的微生物组组成变化数据，也未明确展示这些免疫变化是由特定的微生物组改变介导并直接导致临床疗效提升的统计关联。其机制推断依赖于将患者粪便移植到小鼠模型中的间接证据。\\n\\n2. **微生物组改变与临床疗效脱节**：在BEWELL研究（肺癌，多酚干预）中，黑树莓花蜜干预显著增加了*Lachnospiraceae*丰度，且这种微生物组变化在小鼠模型中证实能增强ICI疗效。但该研究未报告接受干预的人类患者自身的ICI临床疗效数据，因此无法确认人类体内的微生物组改变是否直接转化为临床获益。\\n\\n此外，另一项提案（Shaverdashvili et al., 2025）旨在通过高发酵食品和高纤维补充来调节微生物组和免疫参数，但目前仅为研究计划，无结果数据。因此，当前证据显示饮食干预可改变微生物组或免疫表型，但尚未在人类队列中直接证实“饮食->微生物组->免疫->疗效”这一完整因果链的直接临床关联。\",\n  \"decision\": \"external_research\",\n  \"still_missing\": \"需要找到一项已完成的人类饮食干预临床试验，该试验必须同时满足以下条件：1) 患者接受ICI治疗；2) 报告了干预前后具体的肠道微生物组组成变化（如特定菌属/物种丰度变化）；3) 提供了这些微生物组变化与患者临床疗效（如ORR、PFS、OS）之间的直接统计关联或因果分析证据。\",\n  \"read_focus\": \"重点检索DIET试验（NCT04645680）的全文发表版本或其他相关出版物，查看是否包含人类参与者微生物组测序数据及其与疗效的相关性分析。同时检索BEWELL研究（NCT04267874）的最终临床结果报告。\",\n  \"external_ask\": \"请检索是否有已发表的文献报告了人类饮食干预试验中，微生物组的具体变化（如菌群多样性、特定菌属丰度）与ICI治疗临床疗效（如响应率、生存期）之间的直接关联数据。\",\n  \"reason\": \"虽然找到了人类饮食干预试验（DIET和BEWELL），但它们分别只提供了免疫表型变化或微生物组变化，且均通过动物实验间接推断疗效，或缺乏人类临床疗效的微生物组关联数据。未能直接回答“是否通过改变微生物组直接改善了ICI疗效”这一因果链条问题。\"\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Here's a thinking process:\n\n1.  **Analyze User Input:**\n    *   **Gap ID:** N703f55efd0f8\n    *   **Intended Use:** mechanism\n    *   **Question:** 在人类 ICI 治疗队列中，是否有干预性研究证实特定饮食模式（如高纤维）通过改变微生物组直接改善了 ICI 疗效？ (In human ICI treatment cohorts, are there interventional studies confirming that specific dietary patterns (e.g., high fiber) directly improve ICI efficacy by altering the microbiome?)\n    *   **Success Criteria:** Find 1 human diet intervention trial reporting a causal chain between microbiome changes and ICI efficacy, or confirm current evidence is only observational associations.\n    *   **User Scope:** How does the gut microbiome modulate the efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors in solid tumours, and what mechanisms mediate that effect?\n    *   **Materials Found:** 3 papers/abstracts provided.\n        *   Paper 1 (2625c...): DIET trial abstract (AACR 2026). Phase II RCT in melanoma patients on ICB. High fiber (30-50g/d) vs control (20g/d). Reports peripheral immune profiling (reduced monocytes/macrophages, increased T cell activation). Includes human-to-mouse FMT showing improved ICB response with post-intervention stool. Notes circulating monocyte levels negatively correlate with ICB response. *Crucially:* Does not report direct human clinical efficacy outcomes linked to microbiome changes in this abstract. Relies on mouse FMT for efficacy link.\n        *   Paper 2 (1313c...): Proposal abstract (IO 2025). Prospective trial design for sequential high-fermented food + high-fiber in ICI-treated melanoma. No outcome data yet.\n        *   Paper 3 (4b207...): BEWELL study abstract (ESMO Open 2024). RCT crossover in lung cancer patients (not explicitly stated as receiving ICI concurrently in the abstract text, but mentions ICB context). Black raspberry nectar vs placebo. Shows increase in Lachnospiraceae. Efficacy validated via syngeneic mouse models (MC38, CMT167) treated with anti-PD1 receiving human stool. *Crucially: